.Dd August 24, 2026
.Dt HEDL 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm hedl
.Nd dwl with a script and a socket
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl v
.Op Fl d
.Op Fl s Ar startup command
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a Wayland compositor based on wlroots, and a soft fork of
.Nm dwl ,
which fills the place
.Xr dwm 1
holds on X11.
.Pp
The window model is dwl's and is not changed.
Tags, not workspaces.
Master and stack.
Client rules.
No bar, no decoration, no launcher.
.Pp
What changes is the seam.
dwm and dwl put the configuration in
.Pa config.h ,
so every change is a fork and every request is a patch.
.Nm
hands you a language instead: the configuration is a Lua file, and the user is
still the programmer.
Layouts, rules and event handlers are written there rather than compiled in.
.Pp
The other half is the protocol.
.Nm
publishes what it knows as lines, kind first, and reads commands as lines in the
same shape.
It owns windows, tags, focus and layout, and nothing else.
A bar, a menu or a script is another program that reads a socket.
.Pp
.Nm
keeps upstream's file layout so that merges stay cheap.
.Pa src/dwl.c
is upstream's file in upstream's order, and every divergence carries a comment:
.Dl grep \-n \(aqHEDL:\(aq src/*.h src/*.c
.Ss Options
When given the
.Fl v
option,
.Nm
writes its name and version to standard error and exits unsuccessfully.
.Pp
When given the
.Fl d
option,
.Nm
enables full wlroots logging, including debug information.
.Pp
When given the
.Fl s
option,
.Nm
starts a shell process running
.Ar command
when starting.
The status is written to the standard input of that process instead of to
standard output.
A command that does not read the status must close its standard input, or
.Nm
blocks.
When stopping,
.Nm
sends
.Dv SIGTERM
to the child process group and waits for it to exit.
.Sh CONFIGURATION
.Nm
reads
.Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/hedl/hedl.lua
at start and again on the
.Em reload
dispatcher, where
.Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
falls back to
.Pa ~/.config .
.Ev HEDL_CONFIG
names another file.
The search path for
.Fn require
is that same directory and then
.Pa $PREFIX/share/hedl .
.Pp
With no file of your own,
.Nm
reads the one it ships, at
.Pa $PREFIX/share/hedl/hedl.lua .
Copy it to start from it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mkdir -p ~/.config/hedl
cp /usr/share/hedl/hedl.lua ~/.config/hedl/
.Ed
.Pp
A Lua configuration replaces the whole key table, so what one binds is
everything the keyboard does, the VT switches included.
If a file has a syntax error, or binds no key, the binds from before it stay in
charge, and
.Pa config.h
if there were none.
A bad configuration cannot leave the session without a keyboard.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
hedl.config({
  general    = { border_size = 2 },
  colors     = { focus = "#7aa2f7", border = "#3b4261" },
  decoration = { active_opacity = 1.0, inactive_opacity = 0.92 },
  animation  = { enabled = true, divisor = 6, snap = 2 },
  input      = { follow_mouse = true, touchpad = { tap = true } },
})

hedl.bind("SUPER + Return", "Terminal", hedl.dsp.spawn("foot"))
hedl.window_rule({ class = "^steam$" }, { tile = true })
hedl.monitor_rule("eDP-1", { scale = 1.5, layout = "tile" })
hedl.on("map", function(c) print(c.title) end)
hedl.layout("dwindle", function(ctx) ... end)
.Ed
.Pp
A dispatcher is a value.
.Fn hedl.dsp.focusstack 1
returns an object holding a function and its argument, and the same object goes
to a key bind, to a rule, or to the command channel.
A key press and a command line share one parser as a result.
The dispatchers are
.Em chvt ,
.Em focusmon ,
.Em focusstack ,
.Em incnmaster ,
.Em killclient ,
.Em moveresize ,
.Em quit ,
.Em reload ,
.Em setlayout ,
.Em setmfact ,
.Em spawn ,
.Em tag ,
.Em tagmon ,
.Em togglefloating ,
.Em togglefullscreen ,
.Em toggletag ,
.Em toggleview ,
.Em view
and
.Em zoom .
.Pp
A layout function is called once for each arrange, with the usable area of the
monitor,
.Em nmaster ,
.Em mfact
and the visible tiled clients.
C keeps the box math, Lua decides which box goes where.
A layout that runs for more than 2000000 instructions is stopped, named on
standard error, and replaced with
.Em tile .
.Pp
See
.Pa README.md
for the whole interface.
.Ss Default key bindings
These are the bindings in the shipped
.Pa hedl.lua ,
and in
.Pa config.h ,
which runs when neither Lua file is there.
They might differ depending on your keyboard layout.
.Bl -tag -width 20n -offset indent -compact
.It Mod-[1-9]
Show only all windows with a tag.
.It Mod-Ctrl-[1-9]
Show all windows with a tag.
.It Mod-Shift-[1-9]
Move window to a single tag.
.It Mod-Ctrl-Shift-[1-9]
Toggle tag for window.
.It Mod-p
Spawn
.Xr wmenu-run 1 .
.It Mod-Shift-Return
Spawn
.Xr foot 1 .
.It Mod-[jk]
Move focus down/up the stack.
.It Mod-[id]
Increase/decrease number of windows in master area.
.It Mod-[hl]
Decrease/increase master area.
.It Mod-Return
Move window on top of stack or switch top of stack with second window.
.It Mod-Tab
Show only all windows with previous tag.
.It Mod-Shift-c
Close window.
.It Mod-t
Switch to tiled layout.
.It Mod-f
Switch to floating layout.
.It Mod-m
Switch to monocle layout.
.It Mod-Space
Switch to previous layout.
.It Mod-Shift-Space
Toggle floating state of window.
.It Mod-e
Toggle fullscreen state of window.
.It Mod-0
Show all windows.
.It Mod-Shift-0
Set all tags for window.
.It Mod-,
Move focus to previous monitor.
.It Mod-.
Move focus to next monitor.
.It Mod-Shift-,
Move window to previous monitor.
.It Mod-Shift-.
Move window to next monitor.
.It Mod-Shift-q
Quit
.Nm .
.El
.Ss Mouse commands
.Bl -tag -width 20n -offset indent -compact
.It Mod-Button1
Move focused window while dragging.
Tiled windows will be toggled to the floating state.
.It Mod-Button2
Toggle focused window between floating and tiled state.
.It Mod-Button3
Resize focused window while dragging.
Tiled windows will be toggled to the floating state.
.El
.Sh STATUS INFORMATION
.Nm
writes its status in the kipp format: the kind of fact, then the subject, then
.Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value
attributes, tab separated.
One line for each fact.
This replaces dwl's status output, so dwlb and other dwl bars do not read it.
.Pp
The status goes to standard output, or to the child of
.Fl s ,
and to a listening socket at
.Pa $HEDLDIR/kipp .
A reader that connects gets a version line, the whole picture, the key bindings,
and then
.Sy sync .
It gets the whole picture again on every change.
Nothing is queued and nothing is answered.
.Pp
The kinds are:
.Bl -tag -width 8n -offset indent -compact
.It Sy version
The protocol version.
.It Sy mon
An output, with its width and height.
.It Sy focus
The selected monitor.
.It Sy tags
Used, selected and urgent tags of a monitor, each a comma separated list of tag
numbers.
.It Sy layout
The name of the layout of a monitor.
.It Sy title
The title of the focused window on a monitor, or nothing.
.It Sy app
The app id of the focused window on a monitor, or nothing.
.It Sy win
Fullscreen and floating state of the focused window on a monitor.
.It Sy cap
A window a screen recorder can point at, named by its
ext-foreign-toplevel-list identifier.
One line for each window, not for each monitor.
.It Sy bind
A key the configuration bound, with its description.
Published when the configuration loads and when a reader connects.
.It Sy sync
The end of the opening dump.
.El
.Ss Examples
.Bd -literal -offset indent
version	1	hedl	proto=1
mon	eDP\-1	w=2560	h=1440
focus	eDP\-1
tags	eDP\-1	used=1,3	sel=1	urgent=
layout	eDP\-1	name=tile
title	eDP\-1	text=README.md
app	eDP\-1	id=foot
win	eDP\-1	fullscreen=0	floating=0
cap	1a2b3c	mon=eDP\-1	app=foot	title=README.md
bind	SUPER + Return	desc=Terminal
sync	state
.Ed
.Sh COMMANDS
.Nm
reads commands from a FIFO at
.Pa $HEDLDIR/cmd .
One command for each line, in the same tab separated format, dispatched through
the registry the keyboard uses:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
printf \(aqview\et2\en\(aq > "$HEDLDIR/cmd"
.Ed
.Pp
Anything that can write a line can drive the window manager.
There is no library, no handshake and no generated protocol.
.Sh EFFECTS
There are two.
Per-window opacity, active and inactive, with pattern lists that hold a window
always opaque or always translucent.
And one animation curve,
.Li x += (target \- x) / divisor ,
which snaps to the target inside a few pixels.
It is stepped where the output already draws, so it costs a frame that was
already being drawn.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
These environment variables are used by
.Nm :
.Bl -tag -width XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
.It Ev XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
A directory where temporary user files, such as the Wayland socket,
are stored.
.It Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where per-user configuration lives.
.Nm
reads
.Pa hedl/hedl.lua
under it.
A relative value is read as unset, and the fallback is
.Pa ~/.config .
.It Ev XDG_CONFIG_DIR
A directory containing configuration of various programs and
libraries, including libxkbcommon.
.It Ev HEDL_CONFIG
The Lua configuration file to read, instead of
.Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/hedl/hedl.lua .
.It Ev HEDLDIR
The directory holding the status socket and the command FIFO, instead of
.Pa $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/hedl .
Set it to run a nested instance.
.It Ev DISPLAY , WAYLAND_DISPLAY , WAYLAND_SOCKET
Tell how to connect to an underlying X11 or Wayland server.
.It Ev WLR_*
Various variables specific to wlroots.
.It Ev XKB_* , XLOCALEDIR , XCOMPOSEFILE
Various variables specific to libxkbcommon.
.It Ev XCURSOR_PATH
List of directories to search for XCursor themes in.
.It Ev HOME
A directory where there are always dear files there for you.
Waiting for you to clean them up.
.El
.Pp
These are set by
.Nm :
.Bl -tag -width WAYLAND_DISPLAY
.It Ev WAYLAND_DISPLAY
Tell how to connect to
.Nm .
.It Ev HEDLDIR
The directory it settled on, for the processes it spawns.
.It Ev DISPLAY
If using
.Nm Xwayland ,
tell how to connect to the
.Nm Xwayland
server.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Start
.Nm
with a terminal server that does not read the status:
.Dl hedl \-s \(aqfoot \-\-server <&\-\(aq
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dwm 1 ,
.Xr foot 1 ,
.Xr wmenu 1 ,
.Xr xkeyboard-config 7
.Sh BUGS
All of them.
